── WHAT A FRESH SESSION MUST KNOW ──
1. SYNC IS WORSE THAN OLD NOTES SAY, and the instrument was why. Every sync number quoted before
   2026-08-23 was ~31 ms optimistic: the oracle carried an exact +18.00 ms detection bias (half a
   30 fps frame + half a 128-sample envelope window) and never measured the VIDEO reference at all
   (13.5 ms) while correcting the audio one. CI now gates the SYMMETRIC metric at ≤90 ms and reads
   40-66 ms. The instrument's own noise floor is ~8-9 ms 1σ — no band below that is measurable.
   O4's job is to bring this to ≤20 ms; re-tighten the band then.
2. THE AUDIO ANCHOR CANNOT SEE INPUT LATENCY. It dates sample 0 from when a worklet batch ARRIVES,
   so device/stream buffering is invisible and audio lands late. Loopback-measured at +128.7 ms
   (sd 0.70); dating from the audio clock instead changes it by 1.1 ms, so this is signal-path, not
   messaging. Production now subtracts the platform-reported track latency (bounded, logged, never a
   fitted constant — a 90 ms fallback was rejected on those grounds in 2026-07).
3. NOTHING IN ARMING MAY AWAIT WITHOUT A DEADLINE. Each step was bounded but the joins were not, and
   that froze recording with a stale "Waiting for microphone…" label. Budgets now bound the joins,
   every durable-worker round trip has a deadline, the record button cancels an arm, and a pagehide
   guard releases devices. Keep it that way.
4. EVIDENCE IS BROWSER-SIDE. Unit tests cover pure logic only; anything about capture, export,
   memory or bundles must be measured in a real browser on the PRODUCTION build. QA on the prod-build
   port only, never the dev server.

